2025-08-05 23:30:14,218 - INFO - Here is an alternate way to close the gap between the two drawings without simply “nudging” each object again.  Think of the illustration as three logical blocks –  

BLOCK A (left-hand stack of circles),  
BLOCK B (the green vertical oval) and  
BLOCK C (the thin yellow bar).  

Instead of editing every element separately, tighten–up the relationship between the blocks themselves and standardise a few global style rules:

-------------------------------------------------
1. Adopt a “no external stroke” rule for all three filled circles  
   • Add a global CSS/style section (or a group style) that sets stroke-width to 0 for any element whose fill is purple, blue or orange.  
   • This instantly eliminates the unwanted black hair-line without touching each circle’s coordinates.

2. Align BLOCK A to the top margin as a group, not per-circle  
   • Wrap the three circles in a <g> element.  
   • Move the whole group upward until the purple circle’s top is almost kissing the canvas edge (leave roughly the thickness of the yellow bar as margin).  
   • Inside the group keep the existing vertical spacing so the blue and orange circles ride up automatically.

3. Vertically pin BLOCK B to BLOCK A rather than to the page  
   • Still inside the circles’ group, add an invisible horizontal reference line at the bottom of the purple circle.  
   • Give the green oval a y-coordinate that exactly matches that reference line.  
   • This ensures the oval’s top will always sit level with the bottom of the purple circle—even if you later tweak sizes.

4. Re-anchor BLOCK C from its RIGHT edge instead of its LEFT  
   • Set the yellow bar’s x position as “canvas-width – bar-width – small-margin”, so its right end always stays the same distance from the canvas edge.  
   • Now adjust the bar’s width so its left end sits one green-oval-stroke away from the oval.  
   • Finally change its stroke colour style to the same bright blue used for the bar’s outline in the target.

5. Pull BLOCK C slightly closer to the very top  
   • Give the bar a top margin equal to one quarter of its own height.  
   • Because the circles were moved as a group in step 2, their relative clearance below the bar will now mimic the target automatically.

-------------------------------------------------
Why this alternative strategy may succeed where previous pixel-nudges failed

• By grouping items and aligning groups instead of single objects, you cut the number of positions to maintain from six down to three, so subsequent tweaks won’t knock things out of line again.  
• A global “stroke-width:0” rule is simpler and more robust than trying to recolour each stroke to match its fill.  
• Anchoring the yellow bar from its right end stops the left edge from drifting if you later adjust its length.  
• Using “relative-to-other-block” positions (oval top aligned with purple bottom) guarantees vertical harmony even if circle sizes are tuned later.
